      "source": "authorised",
      "id": "owner-aug15-chemistry-chemistry-100-md-49",
      "subject": "Chemistry",
      "field": "explanation",
      "value": "This restates the same principle as Q21: a hydrated salt (here, sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na₂CO₃·10H�2O) spontaneously losing water of crystallization to the atmosphere without external heating is efflorescence. Deliquescence is the opposite process (absorbing atmospheric moisture until the solid dissolves), hygroscopy is absorbing moisture without full dissolution, and effervescence refers to gas bubbling, unrelated to hydration state changes. This concept appears in multiple JAMB years — worth memorizing thoroughly given its recurrence.\n\n---"
    },
